etiquette_feedback:
The email follows a clear structure with a subject line that is concise and relevant to the content of the email. The salutation includes the name of the recipient. The body of the email is brief and to the point, with a clear request and an explanation of the reason for the request. The email contains no spelling or grammar mistakes or excessive punctuation, and includes a professional closing with a signature.}

{
  "email": "Subject: qs : language attitude studies (German)\n\nCan anybody who has done ( or knows about ) current research on language attitudes in a'standard / non-standard variety setting' please contact me? I have just started a thesis on 'attitudes towards \"Hochdeutsch\" in southern Germany' and I would like to find out more about recent ( 1990+ ) research on language attitude studies, especially in the German-speaking area. Thank you, Anne Hof (German Dept., University of Manchester) mflugah@fs1.art.man.ac.uk"
}

This email follows a similar structure and writing quality as the provided email, therefore, it would also be considered sufficient. The only difference is the formatting of the subject line and the use of proper English in the body of the email.

